# borer development rules, for every contributor and coding agent. The agent
# files (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, AGENT.md, GEMINI.md, QWEN.md, .rules,
# .cursorrules, .windsurfrules, .clinerules, .roorules, .junie/guidelines.md,
# .github/copilot-instructions.md) only point here: edit this file. Valid YAML.
# Sections run most critical first. A rule naming a tool is enforced by it.

about: 'borer is an agent-native reverse-engineering engine: borer-engine, a pure, sync, wasm-buildable Rust library that owns its loader, IR and analysis, and borer, a thin MCP server and CLI over it. The agent is the user, so token economy is a feature. No GUI, web UI or TUI, ever; never bridge to another reverse-engineering tool as a backend.'
critical:
  simplicity:
  - Fewest lines that work. Same behavior in less code wins; nothing clever, nothing cute.
  - 'No abstraction without a second user: no trait, generic, wrapper or layer that only forwards.'
  - Search before writing (rg for the name and the shape). Use std or the existing helper; the second copy of any logic becomes a shared helper in the module that owns that concern.
  - Smallest change that solves the problem. No drive-by refactors, no speculative options.
  - Delete dead code. No commented-out code, compatibility shims or dead feature flags.
  - Functions stay within 100 code lines and files within 1500 (scripts/check/sizes.py; entries in sizes.tsv may shrink, never grow). Duplication stays under the .jscpd.json threshold.
  correctness:
  - Never claim more than the engine shows. A heuristic answer names its evidence and says when it is unsure; a missed finding beats a false one. Rough output says it is rough.
  - 'Determinism is a contract: same binary, same query, same bytes, at any thread count. No clock (clippy.toml disallowed-methods), seed, hash order (iter_over_hash_type) or environment in an answer.'
  - Never guess a crate API; read docs.rs or ~/.cargo/registry first.
  - Done means a command says done. Check the command's own exit status, never a pipe's (`gate.py > log 2>&1 && git commit`).
  - 'Never edit a test or a benchmark to pass; report the wrong one. Every fix comes with a test: a decompiler fix in tests/it/decompile.rs, a lifter fix as an *_agrees_with_the_emulator test.'
  - 'Push back on a wrong idea, whoever it comes from: say why, then do the right thing or ask.'
  untrusted_input:
  - Bytes may be malformed, packed or hostile. Every read is bounds-checked, every scan capped, every walk fuel-limited. Fed garbage, borer answers or errors; it never panics, hangs or balloons.
  - No unwrap, expect, panic!, unreachable! or todo! outside tests (clippy, deny). Arithmetic on values from the file is checked, saturating or wrapping (fuzz builds trap overflow).
  - Every parser is reachable from a fuzz target (fuzz/, CI). A crashing input becomes a test in tests/hostile.rs.
  - unsafe lives only in borer-live and tests, each block under a `// SAFETY:` line (unsafe_code, undocumented_unsafe_blocks).
  workflow:
  - Work lands on dev without pull requests: work on a `type/topic` branch in its own worktree, one concern per commit; before pushing, run both `greptile review -b dev --agent` and `coderabbit review --agent --committed --base dev -c CLAUDE.md` on the branch and fix or answer every finding (if one service stays down after retries, say so and land on the other), then fast-forward dev to it and push. No pull requests into main either: main moves by fast-forwarding it to dev and pushing, and that push runs the full gate and CI; a red main is fixed on dev and fast-forwarded again.
  - Parallel agents never share a working tree, and never use git stash (all worktrees share one stack).
  - 'Commit titles: `type: summary`, lowercase, imperative, plain ASCII, at most 72 characters, no scope, no period; only Co-Authored-By trailers below. Types: add, fix, improve, refactor, perf, docs, test, chore, revert (scripts/check/commits.py).'
  - One commit, one concern, green at every commit. Never commit build output, packs or ignored files.
  gate:
  - '`python scripts/gate.py` is the termination test; green before every commit, or say gate not run. CI on dev runs only the quick checks (format, commits, comments, typos, ruff, clippy); the full `--check` gate, the Linux/macOS/arm64 tests and fuzzing run on a pull request into main, a push to main and a manual run; `--fast` skips the slow tail; passed steps are cached; one gate runs per machine.'
  - Never silence a lint. Fix it, or put `#[expect(lint, reason = "...")]` on the one site; `#[allow]` is denied (allow_attributes).
  - 'Lint levels live in Cargo.toml [workspace.lints]: lints that flag a bug deny, style warn (an error under the gate''s -D warnings). Tool settings live in clippy.toml, rustfmt.toml, deny.toml, typos.toml, ruff.toml, .jscpd.json and .editorconfig.'
  - 'Builds and borer processes are heavy: at most two borer processes per task, many functions through one process (decompile_batch), one build at a time.'
important:
  engine:
  - 'borer-engine is pure: sync functions of explicit inputs, no async, tokio, MCP types or filesystem writes (deny.toml bans tokio there).'
  - Program holds only facts from the file and is immutable; user names, comments and types live in an Overlay, identified by its fingerprint.
  - 'Analysis::compute runs once per Program. Every later result is memoized in Analysis::derived, keyed by its inputs; Derived::moved keeps what an edit''s read-set missed, and build threads read through reads::Handle. One memo store: no second Mutex map, no global state.'
  - Per-function parallel work uses std::thread::scope over stripes (analysis::striped), results in address order; rayon stays in the server.
  - 'Decoders sit behind the isa module: iced-x86 (x86/x64), yaxpeax-arm (arm64, only in isa/a64/decode.rs), isa::a32 (A32/T32). decompile::verify checks the lift against the emulator; keep it green.'
  - Target knowledge lives in gitignored packs/*.borer, never as names in engine code.
  server:
  - 'A thin adapter: main.rs holds the tool router and resource dispatch; handlers live per area under tools/; state, params, resources, recall and the CLI have their own modules. Logic belongs in the engine behind a test.'
  - stdout is the protocol (print_stdout); diagnostics go to stderr or into tool results.
  - A failing handler returns ErrorData, which call_tool turns into an isError result (errors::tool_failure). Never error text inside a success.
  - Every list is a plain table with a `#` header line, takes filter, offset, limit and count, and is hard-capped with `showing N of M`. A tool that changes state is a dry run by default.
  - 'Descriptions are for an agent that never saw the project: what it returns, its caps, its defaults.'
  - A parameter that takes one of a few words is a schema enum (params::ReadKind, SymbolKind, SearchKind); no handler matches on strings.
  - Addresses accept 0x-hex, decimal or a name at the boundary (parse_addr, resolve); inside they are u64 virtual addresses.
  types:
  - An address is a u64 virtual address. A file offset or size says so (`off`, `len`, `size`) and converts only through Program::offset_to_va and va_to_offset. Functions and blocks are named by start address and found by Analysis::function or function_at, never by a held index.
  - Parse at the boundary into typed structs, then trust them. Enums over bools, Option over sentinels, usize lengths.
  - 'A width conversion says what it keeps: num::lo32, cast_signed or cast_unsigned, u64::from, try_from, num::as_f64; never an integer `as` (cast lints).'
  - Lifted expressions carry their width (expr::Ty); a width change is an explicit Cast or Sext.
  - Match borer's own enums exhaustively; a wildcard arm only over a foreign enum.
  errors:
  - Option where not-found is the only failure; anyhow::Result where a message must reach the agent, with its context (address, section, what was expected) so the agent can correct itself. No error enum until a caller matches on it.
  - Never drop a Result that matters with `let _ =` or .ok().
  performance:
  - Borrow; never clone to calm the borrow checker. Iterators over index loops, no needless collect, with_capacity when the size is known.
  - 'Cap scans by design: sample, window, page.'
  - 'Bars: a load takes at most 8 bytes of heap per file byte (tests/hostile.rs, gate); a cold decompile is at most 25 ms at p95 and 5 s at worst per function on notepad.exe (tests/bench.rs timings); cleanup never spends its pass budget (dogfood sweep); a request stops at its deadline (BORER_DEADLINE_SECS, 300 s). A perf change shows bench.rs before and after.'
  portability:
  - Windows, Linux and macOS on x64 and arm64 hosts build and pass CI. No host-endian byte conversions (host_endian_bytes); paths go through Path.
  testing:
  - 'cargo test --workspace, the dogfood sweep and scripts/run/smoke.py run in the gate. Engine tests are pure: hand-built bytes and structs, fixtures only when tiny and license-free.'
  - 'Determinism is tested before convenience: the dogfood twice-run and parallel_decompile_matches_sequential in the gate, scripts/run/determinism.py across processes.'
details:
  docs:
  - Every item has rustdoc (missing_docs, missing_docs_in_private_items, cargo doc -D warnings).
  - 'Line 1 is one third-person sentence (`Returns ...`). Then, only those that apply, in order: prose; `# Arguments`, one bullet per non-self parameter with meaning, unit and forms; `# Returns` for a non-unit return, saying what None or Err mean; `# Errors` for a Result; `# Panics` when reachable (a bug in engine or server code); `# Safety` for unsafe; `# Examples` as a doctest on public entry points. No section for show, no restating the signature.'
  - 'Fields, variants and consts get one line: meaning, unit, invariant. Identifiers are backticked and items intra-doc linked (doc_markdown).'
  - No `//` or `/* */` comments except `// SAFETY:` on unsafe and `// ASM:` on hand-assembled bytes (scripts/check/strip_comments.py); the rest belongs in rustdoc or is not written.
  style:
  - Prefer ?, let-else, matches!, map_or, combinators and destructuring. Infer locals; annotate public signatures.
  - 'From and TryFrom for conversions, #[must_use] on builders and results, derived traits over hand-written ones, newtypes for domain values, pub(crate) by default, one responsibility per module, no magic numbers.'
  - rust-toolchain.toml pins the toolchain and rust-version moves with it. A new dependency needs a reason no existing one covers (cargo-deny checks bans, licenses, sources, advisories).
  workspace:
    crates/borer-engine: 'the analysis core: loading, disassembly, lifting, emulation, decompilation, queries.'
    crates/borer: the MCP server over stdio and the CLI.
    crates/borer-live: read-only access to a running process; the only crate calling the OS process API.
    crates/borer-fixtures: small programs the tests compile, decompile and check against their source.
    fuzz: cargo-fuzz targets, outside the workspace.
    scripts: 'the gate (gate.py) and its helpers: check/, run/, dev/, harness/.'
  prefer:
  - avoid: let _ = fallible();
    use: fallible()?;
  - avoid: x as u32
    use: u32::try_from(x)?
  - avoid: a JSON array of 10k strings
    use: strings(filter=.., limit=200) with showing N of M
  - avoid: a Mutex<HashMap> memo on Analysis
    use: analysis.derived.get_or_build(kind, key, || ...)
